---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Aperture from "../components/Aperture.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
const notes = (await getCollection("notes")).sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf());
const featured = notes[0];
---
<Base title="Waking Matter" description="Building systems for agents that improve with experience. Memory, provenance and evaluation for repeated work in a changing world." canonical="https://wakingmatter.com/" tone="void" current="home">
  <main id="content">
    <div class="hero-band">
      <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="label hero-eyebrow">Building systems for cumulative intelligence</p>
          <h1 id="hero-title">Agents should improve with experience.</h1>
          <p class="hero-deck">We are building systems to help agents retain useful experience, act on changing knowledge, and become more capable across repeated work.</p>
          <a class="hero-cta" href="#memory">The problem <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-figure"><Aperture class="hero-aperture" /></div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section id="memory" class="band" aria-labelledby="memory-title">
      <div class="wrap split">
        <p class="label">The problem</p>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="memory-title">Memory is not enough.</h2>
          <p>Retaining information is not the same as learning from experience. The harder question is whether yesterday’s work changes today’s decisions for the better.</p>
          <p>What should an agent retrieve, trust, test or try differently? When should it forget, reset, stop or ask a person? More history can help, do nothing, or make the next decision worse. Improvement has to be checked.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" aria-labelledby="questions-label">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="band-head"><p class="label" id="questions-label">Questions we are working on</p><p class="label">01 / 04</p></div>
        <div class="trait-grid question-grid">
          <article class="trait"><span class="meta num">01</span><h2>Experience into action</h2><p>How should prior experience change the next allocation of retrieval, attention, compute and action?</p></article>
          <article class="trait"><span class="meta num">02</span><h2>Memory into useful state</h2><p>What should persist, in what form, and for how long? How can its source and limits remain visible?</p></article>
          <article class="trait"><span class="meta num">03</span><h2>Changing worlds</h2><p>How should a system notice that inherited knowledge is stale or harmful, and decide what to revise or reset?</p></article>
          <article class="trait"><span class="meta num">04</span><h2>Evaluation into improvement</h2><p>Does experience improve the outcome, or did we just add context, compute or advice the system already had?</p></article>
        </div>
        <a class="text-link" href="/research/">Explore the research questions <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" aria-labelledby="work-title">
      <div class="wrap split">
        <p class="label">Public technical work</p>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="work-title">Agent Memory Contracts</h2>
          <p>An agent’s interpretation should not silently become a fact. Agent Memory Contracts is a public Python library that makes the boundary between extracted claims and trusted memory explicit.</p>
          <p>Source evidence stays linked to a candidate claim. Accepting that claim requires an explicit decision. The validator rejects a trusted record without the required authorization. The <a href="https://github.com/avs-io/agent-memory-contracts/blob/main/examples/reference_reducer.py">worked reducer example</a> shows acceptance, rejection and an invalid promotion.</p>
          <p>This is a foundation for reliable learning. The next test is whether agents use that memory to make better decisions across repeated work.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/avs-io/agent-memory-contracts">Inspect the code and tests <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" aria-labelledby="workflows-title">
      <div class="wrap split">
        <p class="label">Repeated work</p>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="workflows-title">The test is whether this matters in real work.</h2>
          <p>We are interested in bounded workflows where agents rediscover context, repeat mistakes or struggle when facts change. Outcomes should be checkable, uncertainty visible, and human escalation explicit.</p>
          <p>The aim is for repeated work to become measurably better without agents becoming more confidently wrong. We are early, building and testing, and looking for hard problems and collaborators.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="mailto:hello@wakingmatter.com">Bring a recurring problem <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" aria-labelledby="thesis-title">
      <div class="wrap split">
        <p class="label">The broader thesis</p>
        <div class="section-copy"><h2 id="thesis-title">Intelligence should accumulate.</h2><p>Useful learning should survive a session and compound across work, agents, models and time. Keeping it useful, revisable and worth inheriting is the work of cumulative intelligence.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {featured && <section class="band notes-teaser" aria-labelledby="notes-label"><div class="wrap"><p class="label" id="notes-label">Notes</p><a class="note-card" href={`/notes/${featured.slug}/`}><span class="meta">{featured.data.pubDate.toISOString().slice(0, 10)}</span><span class="note-card-title">{featured.data.title}</span><p>{featured.data.description}</p></a></div></section>}
  </main>
</Base>
